WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.190
ROGER, short for Retrospective Observational Cohort Generator for Epidemiology Research, is a workspace for designing and running observational studies.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:18.000
We built it so investigators can see how their study decisions are translated into code and check the outputs along the way.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:29.330
For this demonstration, we used Medicare data to look for existing medicines that might reduce hospitalizations for flare-ups of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, or COPD.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:36.630
We started with a medication screen to identify candidates, then followed up one of those candidates in a treatment comparison.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.200
The workflow starts by importing our study protocol into ROGER.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:51.720
We used HARPER, the Harmonized Protocol Template to Enhance Reproducibility, to document the study population, treatments, outcomes, and analysis plan.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:58.050
ROGER proposes study settings from the protocol, and I review those settings alongside the source text.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:06.100
For example, I open the hospitalization definition and check that the diagnosis codes and follow-up window match what we specified.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:12.880
Any corrections or unresolved decisions can be recorded here, so there's a record of what changed and why.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.750
Once I've reviewed the definitions and code lists, I confirm that review before ROGER fills in the study settings.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.410
Those reviewed settings are then passed into the code used to build the cohort.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:32.550
I open the generated cohort program to check how it applies the eligibility rules and identifies the hospitalizations we defined.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:40.290
ROGER supports SAS, R, and Python workflows, and validation of the code for each method remains in progress.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:46.780
Once the program is ready, I submit the cohort job to the institution's configured server or workspace.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.650
The execution log shows its progress and any errors that need attention.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:58.750
When cohort construction finishes, the attrition table shows how many patients remain after each eligibility rule.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:06.640
If a rule removes more patients than expected, I can trace that drop back to the definition and code before proceeding with the screen.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.460
With the screening data prepared, I select iDEAS in ROGER and run the medication screen.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:23.500
iDEAS is an empirical Bayes model that compares outcomes before and after medication initiation, borrowing information across medications to identify potential repurposing signals.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:32.030
In the results table, furosemide, a loop diuretic, was associated with fewer COPD hospitalizations after initiation.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:38.750
Opening its row shows the estimate and uncertainty interval, along with the number of patients contributing to the signal.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:49.120
That finding led us to ask whether adding furosemide to inhaled COPD maintenance treatment was associated with fewer hospitalizations than continuing maintenance treatment alone.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:58.620
In ROGER, we define one strategy as starting furosemide within thirty days of study entry and the other as avoiding loop diuretics during that period.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:12.980
ROGER's clone, censor, weight target trial emulation copies each patient into both strategies, stops following copies after a treatment deviation or missed initiation deadline, and weights the remaining records to account for that selection.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:19.590
Follow-up begins at the same study entry point for both strategies and continues for up to one hundred eighty days.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:27.990
I review those treatment definitions, the initiation deadline, and the hospitalization outcome in the analysis screen before submitting the comparison.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:33.220
Before interpreting the hospitalization estimate, I open the weight diagnostics for this run.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:39.680
The weight distribution and effective sample size help show whether a small number of patients have a lot of influence.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.070
Another question is whether adjustment has made the treatment groups more comparable.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:52.340
Across its analysis methods, ROGER provides Table One summaries and balance plots to examine differences in measured patient characteristics.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:04:00.140
For methods using propensity scores, it also plots patients' estimated chances of receiving treatment, so we can see where the groups overlap.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:09.440
To check how much a design choice affects the result, we can change a setting such as follow-up length, run a separate sensitivity analysis, and retain both versions.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:17.460
In the hospitalization figure, the point estimate favored the furosemide strategy, with an interval odds ratio of about zero point eight.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.400
The ninety-five percent confidence interval ranged from zero point six four to zero point nine nine six.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:31.340
I open the numerical table beneath the figure to check the exact values, and the saved analysis settings show how that result was produced.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:39.060
To use the figure in a presentation, I edit its title, labels, and caption to make the treatment comparison and reported measure clear.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:46.820
The figure can then be exported with the underlying tables and a report, and I can also save the program used for the completed run.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:53.564
A colleague reviewing those files can follow the result back through the code and study settings to the protocol we started with.
